{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that masterfully weaves together the story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous lives of its founders. directed by david fincher, the movie is a riveting and thought-provoking exploration of the complexities of human relationships, the cutthroat world of technology, and the consequences of ambition.\n\nthe film's narrative is presented in a non-linear fashion, jumping back and forth between different periods in the lives of mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg) and his associates. this non-linear structure adds to the sense of tension and complexity, as the audience is forced to piece together the events that led to the creation of facebook and the subsequent fallout.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a nuanced and captivating performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character's intelligence, awkwardness, and ruthless determination. his portrayal is both fascinating and unsettling, as he brings to life a character that is both relatable and repulsive. andrew garfield and justin timberlake also shine in their respective roles as eduardo saverin and sean parker, bringing depth and complexity to their characters.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its exploration of the themes of friendship, betrayal, and the corrupting influence of power. as mark zuckerberg's success grows, so too does his isolation and disconnection from those around him. the film raises important questions about the costs of ambition and the true nature of friendship in the face of adversity.\n\nthe film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with a distinctive visual style that captures the sterile, high-tech world of harvard and the silicon valley startup culture. the use of lighting and camera angles adds to the sense of tension and unease, creating a visually stunning and immersive experience.\n\nthe social network 